# What the source distribution carries beyond the package itself (#84).
#
# setuptools puts the modules found under `src/`, the readme, the licence file
# and its own metadata into an sdist, and nothing else. An archive built without
# the lines below carried `src/` and `tests/` and no `catalogue/`, no `tools/`,
# no workflow files and none of the data the package reads, so the suite inside
# it could not be run from it. That is the second half of what #84 measured.
#
# The package data itself is not listed here. It is declared once, in
# `[tool.setuptools.package-data]` in pyproject.toml, and setuptools carries a
# declared package-data file into both distributions from that one declaration.
# A second spelling of the same list here is the drift this repository keeps
# finding, so the sdist and the wheel agree by construction rather than by
# somebody remembering both.
#
# WHAT IS STILL NOT RUNNABLE FROM AN UNPACKED ARCHIVE, and it is not a file this
# list forgot. tests/test_type_check_coverage.py and tests/test_coverage_bar.py
# derive what they are about from `git ls-files`, because a list of modules kept
# by hand falls behind the tree in silence and neither test would notice. An
# unpacked sdist is not a repository, so both refuse there whatever this file
# carries, and the repair for that would be to replace the tree query with a
# maintained list, which is the failure those two exist against.
graft tests
graft tools
graft catalogue
graft docs
graft security
graft .github
include .python-version
include .dockerignore
include requirements.lock
include requirements-dev.lock
include requirements-package.lock
include requirements-scan.lock

# Build products and caches, in case one is sitting in the tree when the archive
# is made. An sdist carrying a compiled module from somebody's working copy is a
# distribution whose contents depend on the machine that built it.
global-exclude *.py[cod]
prune **/__pycache__
